From c2e5b7131743465b789e446ca7095a0256388b04 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: zed-patches <zed-patches@localhost>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 17:20:31 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] agent_ui, sidebar: thread lists belong to a project or are
 global
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Zeo next proposal §6.49 (ask 73), the same two scopes pins have had since
§6.5. While a project is active, the Lists view shows that project's lists
first, under "This Project", then the global lists under "Global"; another
project's lists are hidden until it is active, as 0106 hides its pins.
Without a project list to show, the view keeps its single, unheaded run of
lists, so nothing changes for anyone who never makes one.

"New List" makes a list of the active project, as a plain "Pin" pins to it,
and "New Global List" sits beside it while a project is active. "New List…"
from a thread's menu makes the list in the thread's project when that is
the active one (so the list is shown and the filing allowed), global
otherwise. "Move to List" offers the thread's own project's lists and the
global ones, never another project's. A header's menu gains "Make Global"
on a project list and "Move to This Project" on a global list whose every
thread belongs to the active project, so a move never strands a thread in a
list its project cannot show. Move Up/Down step past lists of another scope,
which the view does not show beside this one.

Storage: a domain of its own, ThreadListScopeDb with
thread_list_scope(list_id PRIMARY KEY, project_key), for the reason
pin_scope gives -- 0048's migration list is appended to by 0063 under a USE
flag. A row means "this list belongs to that project"; no row means global,
so every existing list stays global and nothing migrates. Only
ThreadOrganizationStore changes a scope, because it also rewrites the
list's name_key: a global list keeps its lowercase name as the key, as
before; a project list's key is prefixed with its project, so the column's
UNIQUE holds per project and two projects can each have a "Bugs" list,
beside a global one. The store refuses, with ids only in the error, a
filing in another project's list (ListOfAnotherProject) and a move to a
project of a list holding another project's thread (ListHoldsOtherProjects).
Without the scope store every list is global, as before.

Not done: proposal §6.49 also says a thread that changes project leaves a
project list it no longer belongs to. A thread's project is derived from its
worktree paths in ThreadMetadataStore, which this store has no hook on; the
filing stays, visible with the thread's project label, and "Remove from
List" clears it.

The new methods live in thread_organization/list_scope.rs as an impl block,
so thread_organization.rs, already past the 500-line rule, grows only by
the hooks (errors, scoped name check and key, the filing guard, forgetting a
deleted list's scope).

Tests: thread_organization/list_scope_tests.rs (store: scope across a
restart, names per scope with the UNIQUE column on disk, the filing guard,
the move-to-project guard, Make Global against a taken name, forget on
delete, no scope store), lists_view/list_scopes_tests.rs (which lists show
with A, B and no project active; Move Up/Down neighbours) and
sidebar_lists_tests/scopes.rs (the drawn sections). Three tests of 0069
now create their fixture lists in the active project, where "New List"
puts a typed one. Red seen: with other projects' lists shown, the filing
guard open and the key unscoped, exactly the seven tests on those rules
failed.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
